# EVERY SUPPLY WHOSE CAPACITY BUFFER IS FILED AS EMPTY, BESIDE THE EXPOSURE ITS
# OWN TWO MEASUREMENTS IMPLY. One row per layer where the excess demand had nowhere to go.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE ARGUMENT IS ENTIRELY IN THE WORD "STATED". If the filer says the capacity buffer
#    is empty, the supply CANNOT have absorbed the excess by running harder -- there was
#    nothing to run into. So whatever demand stood above the nameplate went unserved, and
#    somebody has to be named as having gone without. An ABSENT capacity slack licenses none
#    of that, because nobody knows whether the supply could have stretched, and a checker
#    built on it would be reporting about nothing.
#
# ✅⛔ IT SAID "MEASURED" AND EVERY ROW IT RETURNS REFUTES THAT. Eight of the eleven are
#    `contractual` and three `intrinsic`; none was observed. A negotiated zero and an
#    entailed zero bind exactly as hard as a counted one -- see entries/stated_zero_capacity.sqlc.
#
# ⭐⭐ `exposure` IS THE ONLY FIGURE IN THIS MODEL WITH NO INSTRUMENT BEHIND IT. Nobody filed
#    it; it is the ceiling the filer's own demand and nameplate imply -- the largest demand
#    against the smallest supply, evaluated at the HIGH CORNER on purpose, because the two
#    sides of a transition fit are anti-correlated and anything summed across the range
#    reports a week that never happened.
#
# ⛔ TWO RULES READ THIS RELATION AND THEY ASK DIFFERENT QUESTIONS OF IT. One wants the layers
#   with a positive exposure and no named holder; the other wants every one of them, against
#   the shares that were admitted. They shared these joins in two inlined copies before this
#   file existed, which is one edit away from two populations under one argument.
-- pm:Nameplate/pm:capacitySlack stated zero, against the layer's own r = n - d.
SELECT r.*
FROM      (
    :compose(entries/stated_zero_capacity.sqlc)
) z
JOIN      (
    :compose(layers/remainder.sqlc)
) r USING (filing, layer)
